WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.429
Welcome. Generation produces a candidate;
verification earns trust. In this

2
00:00:06.429 --> 00:00:12.857
class, you will break polished output into
checkable claims, match

3
00:00:12.857 --> 00:00:19.286
each claim with appropriate evidence,
scale checks to consequence and

4
00:00:19.286 --> 00:00:25.714
change, diagnose failures before editing a
prompt, and compare revisions

5
00:00:25.714 --> 00:00:32.143
on the same representative cases. The goal
is not to

6
00:00:32.143 --> 00:00:38.571
eliminate uncertainty. It is to make
evidence, failures, changes, and

7
00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:45.000
remaining uncertainty visible enough for
an accountable decision.

8
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:51.000
Do not verify a response as one block.
Decompose it.

9
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.000
A factual claim needs an authoritative
source with matching date,

10
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:03.000
version, and scope. A calculation needs
the original inputs, units,

11
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:09.000
formula, and independent recomputation. A
quotation needs the exact original

12
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:15.000
passage and surrounding context. A
classification needs a defined label,

13
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:21.000
representative examples, and an error
review. Code behavior needs execution,

14
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:27.000
tests, and observed postconditions. A
recommendation needs traceable evidence,

15
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:33.000
assumptions, tradeoffs, and qualified
judgment. A claimed action needs

16
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:39.000
target-system evidence that it happened
once, on the correct target,

17
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:45.000
without forbidden effects. Confidence,
fluency, length, and citation-shaped text

18
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:51.000
are not evidence. A second model can help
identify claims

19
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:57.000
or challenge reasoning, but agreement
between generated answers does not

20
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:03.000
replace the nearest primary evidence.
Record each check as pass,

21
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:09.000
fail, unknown, or not applicable. Preserve
the unsupported parts instead

22
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:15.000
of blending them into a confident
paragraph.

23
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.769
Consider this generated statement: the
current service limit is ten

24
00:02:20.769 --> 00:02:26.538
thousand requests per minute, the proposed
workload uses only sixty

25
00:02:26.538 --> 00:02:32.308
percent of capacity, the documentation
says scaling is automatic, and

26
00:02:32.308 --> 00:02:38.077
migration is therefore low risk. That
sentence contains at least

27
00:02:38.077 --> 00:02:43.846
four claims. Open the current official
documentation for the limit

28
00:02:43.846 --> 00:02:49.615
and confirm the account tier and region.
Recompute workload divided

29
00:02:49.615 --> 00:02:55.385
by the applicable limit, including peaks
rather than averages. Compare

30
00:02:55.385 --> 00:03:01.154
the quotation with the exact source and
conditions. Treat low

31
00:03:01.154 --> 00:03:06.923
risk as a judgment, not a fact; examine
dependencies, quotas,

32
00:03:06.923 --> 00:03:12.692
failure modes, rollback, and domain
review. One bad source or

33
00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:18.462
calculation does not make every clause
false, but one correct

34
00:03:18.462 --> 00:03:24.231
number does not prove the conclusion. The
verification table keeps

35
00:03:24.231 --> 00:03:30.000
each result separate and shows which
decision inputs remain unknown.

36
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.250
Choose one AI-generated paragraph. Mark at
least three claim types.

37
00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:42.500
For each, name the nearest evidence that
could prove or

38
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:48.750
disprove it. If no practical evidence is
available, record unknown

39
00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:55.000
rather than inventing a check.

40
00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:02.000
Checkpoint. Two models agree that a
product currently supports a

41
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:09.000
particular feature. Both cite the same
undated community post. The

42
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:16.000
feature controls a production release. Is
that enough evidence to

43
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:23.000
proceed?

44
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:27.500
Do not proceed on that evidence. The
answers are not

45
00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:32.000
independent when they rely on the same
weak source, and

46
00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.500
agreement does not make the post current
or authoritative. Open

47
00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:41.000
the provider's current documentation,
confirm model or product version, region,

48
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:45.500
account tier, interface, and release
status, then perform a bounded

49
00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:50.000
direct test when possible. If the evidence
remains ambiguous, mark

50
00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.500
the capability unknown and stop the
production decision. If you

51
00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:59.000
accepted two-model agreement as
fact-checking, revise the rule:

52
00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:03.500
independent generation is useful for
challenge, but primary evidence and

53
00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:08.000
observation prove volatile product claims.

54
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:14.000
Plan verification before generation. Start
with how the output will

55
00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:20.000
be used and what happens if it is wrong.

56
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:26.000
Low-consequence brainstorming may need a
usefulness and safety review. Current

57
00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:32.000
factual work needs authoritative sources
and claim mapping. Calculations need

58
00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:38.000
independent reconciliation. Code needs
type, build, test, behavior, and security

59
00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:44.000
checks appropriate to its exposure.
Consequential medical, legal, financial,

60
00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:50.000
employment, access, security, safety, or
production decisions need current

61
00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:56.000
evidence, domain expertise, independent
review, authorization, audit, and

62
00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:02.000
recovery. Increase verification when facts
change quickly, model performance is

63
00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:08.000
uncertain, affected people have little
recourse, exposure is wide, or

64
00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:14.000
actions are difficult to reverse. Human
review only counts when

65
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:20.000
the reviewer has the evidence, expertise,
time, authority, and ability

66
00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:26.000
to reject. Define pass criteria and
failure action in advance.

67
00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:32.000
A check without a response to failure is
only an

68
00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:38.000
observation.

69
00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:43.625
When a check fails, diagnose the system
rather than blaming

70
00:08:43.625 --> 00:08:49.250
the prompt by default. A task failure
means the objective

71
00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:54.875
or decision was unclear or unsuitable. A
context failure means

72
00:08:54.875 --> 00:09:00.500
required evidence was missing, stale,
conflicting, poisoned, or outside scope.

73
00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:06.125
A prompt failure means instructions,
constraints, examples, or the output

74
00:09:06.125 --> 00:09:11.750
contract were ambiguous or contradictory.
A model failure concerns capability,

75
00:09:11.750 --> 00:09:17.375
consistency, language, modality, or
behavior on this task. A tool

76
00:09:17.375 --> 00:09:23.000
failure includes wrong parameters,
permissions, execution, timeout, partial

77
00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:28.625
success, or an unverified postcondition. A
review failure means the

78
00:09:28.625 --> 00:09:34.250
test, rubric, sample, reviewer, or
approval process could not detect

79
00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:39.875
the real problem. Record the observed
failure and evidence before

80
00:09:39.875 --> 00:09:45.500
assigning a cause. Choose the smallest
change that addresses the

81
00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:51.125
likely cause while keeping safety and
approval boundaries fixed. More

82
00:09:51.125 --> 00:09:56.750
instructions cannot create missing source
data. A stronger model cannot

83
00:09:56.750 --> 00:10:02.375
repair an unauthorized tool. A prettier
rubric cannot recover an

84
00:10:02.375 --> 00:10:08.000
action that already harmed the wrong
target.

85
00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:15.000
Checkpoint. A summary omits a policy
exception because the supplied

86
00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:22.000
document ended before the exception page.
The prompt was clear.

87
00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:29.000
What should change first: the prompt
wording, the model, or

88
00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:36.000
the context?

89
00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:41.000
Fix the context first. Confirm the
authoritative document is complete,

90
00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:46.000
includes the exception page, and matches
the required version and

91
00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:51.000
scope. Then rerun the same prompt and
claim check. Changing

92
00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:56.000
wording cannot reveal text that was never
supplied. Switching models

93
00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:01.000
may produce a confident guess, which would
hide rather than

94
00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:06.000
solve the failure. If the complete source
is unavailable, record

95
00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:11.000
the policy exception as unknown and
escalate to its owner.

96
00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:16.000
If you selected prompt or model first,
revise the diagnostic

97
00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:21.000
rule: observed missing evidence points to
context before generation quality.

98
00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:27.333
One corrected example is not measured
improvement. Build a small

99
00:12:27.333 --> 00:12:33.667
representative set. Include normal work, a
boundary case, missing data,

100
00:12:33.667 --> 00:12:40.000
conflicting sources, privacy-sensitive
content, and adversarial-like instructions

101
00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:46.333
when relevant. Preserve the original
cases. Version the prompt, context

102
00:12:46.333 --> 00:12:52.667
recipe, output contract, model or product,
tools, and evaluator. Change

103
00:12:52.667 --> 00:12:59.000
one controllable element when practical.
Run old and new versions

104
00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:05.333
on the same cases with the same rubric.
Record passes,

105
00:13:05.333 --> 00:13:11.667
failures, latency or cost when material,
improvements, regressions, and remaining

106
00:13:11.667 --> 00:13:18.000
uncertainty. A revision is better only
when it improves the

107
00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:24.333
intended criterion without unacceptable
loss elsewhere. If safety improves but

108
00:13:24.333 --> 00:13:30.667
useful requests are blocked, measure that
tradeoff. If average quality

109
00:13:30.667 --> 00:13:37.000
rises but one high-consequence boundary
fails, do not hide it

110
00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:43.333
in the average. Decide whether to accept,
revise, roll back,

111
00:13:43.333 --> 00:13:49.667
narrow scope, or stop. Keep the evidence
so later model

112
00:13:49.667 --> 00:13:56.000
or product changes can be retested.

113
00:13:56.000 --> 00:14:02.875
Open the verify-and-improve activity.
Choose a response with at least

114
00:14:02.875 --> 00:14:09.750
three claim types. Record consequence and
nearest evidence for each

115
00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:16.625
claim, then mark pass, fail, unknown, or
not applicable. Classify

116
00:14:16.625 --> 00:14:23.500
every failure as task, context, prompt,
model, tool, or review.

117
00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:30.375
Select one controllable change without
weakening safety or approval. Rerun

118
00:14:30.375 --> 00:14:37.250
the original case and two boundary cases
using the same

119
00:14:37.250 --> 00:14:44.125
checks. Record improvements, regressions,
remaining uncertainty, and a final

120
00:14:44.125 --> 00:14:51.000
decision to accept, revise, roll back,
narrow, or stop.

121
00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:56.000
When you are ready, begin the knowledge
check. You will

122
00:17:56.000 --> 00:18:01.000
choose evidence for a current product
claim, scale verification to

123
00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:06.000
consequence, diagnose missing context,
compare revisions fairly, and distinguish

124
00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:11.000
a measured gain from one persuasive
answer. Review the class

125
00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:16.000
or return to the activity before
submitting. The assessment begins

126
00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:21.000
only when you choose Begin knowledge
check.

127
00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:27.667
Verify claims with the nearest evidence.
Scale checks to consequence.

128
00:18:27.667 --> 00:18:34.333
Diagnose before changing. Compare versions
on the same cases, and

129
00:18:34.333 --> 00:18:41.000
never hide a regression or unknown.
